{
 "$comment": "Hand-written, never generated. It asserts VERDICTS and exit codes, not values: 'M8_length_cv: ok', never 0.578. A golden file full of raw numbers rots on every cosmetic change and trains people to regenerate it unread, so a legitimate threshold change should touch one line here and show up in review as an intent change, while an accidental regression shows up as a verdict flip. THREE fixtures: deck-pass is a well-formed deliverable, deck-broken carries one named defect per page and is readable as a worked example, and deck-degenerate exists only to fail. The third was added in 0.1.390 because ten metrics could not be given a failing case without destroying the second: four are document-WIDE prose properties (every sentence the same length, every title the same shape) that cannot be confined to one labelled page, and six more design defects would have stopped deck-broken teaching anything. Before it, thirteen of eighteen design verdicts and four of seven prose verdicts read ok on BOTH fixtures, so a checker rewritten to `return \"ok\"` would have passed the suite whose stated purpose is to catch that. check_fixtures.py now computes this rather than trusting it: every graded verdict must have a fixture that fails it, and the run prints the count. Seven design metrics are REPORTED rather than graded and cannot fail by construction — they are counted separately, never excused silently, and two of them are exercised through n/a on the degenerate deck, which has no figures to measure.",
